is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRR/BGP routing on router1 (AS1) is down. The bgpd (and FRR) service is not running, so router1 is not learning or advertising any routes beyond its directly connected networks.

Mechanism: When pc1 (195.11.14.2) sends traffic to pc2 (200.1.1.2), it forwards to its gateway router1 (195.11.14.1). Router1 has no route to 200.1.1.0/24, so it immediately returns ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable,” which pc1 sees. Meanwhile, pc2 can still reach its gateway (router2) because router2’s local network is unaffected.

Localization: On router1, “show ip bgp summary” reports “bgpd is not running,” and systemctl shows frr.service is inactive (dead). Router1’s routing table only contains connected routes 193.10.11.0/24 and 195.11.14.0/24; there is no route to 200.1.1.0/24. The pc1→pc2 ping returns “From 195.11.14.1 Destination Net Unreachable,” confirming the response originates at router1. On router2, BGP is up enough to show a neighbor 193.10.11.1 in Active state (no session), consistent with the far side being down.

Root cause: FRR/bgpd was stopped on router1, leaving only connected routes.

Corrective fix: Start FRR on router1 and ensure bgpd/zebra are enabled to load /etc/frr/frr.conf (which already contains the correct neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2 and network 195.11.14.0/24). Commands: systemctl start frr; verify with vtysh “show ip bgp summary” that the eBGP session establishes and that 200.1.1.0/24 is installed in the routing table. === END ===